 When a grant submits its entire permitted prefix, retain the prepared but never-attempted suffix with its original packet numbers, payload spans and reservations. Keep payload source slots stable until that suffix is consumed or discarded. After the flight buffer returns and no request or grant owns the plan, compact consumed metadata and append waiting frames within the existing frame, datagram, byte and turn budgets. Preserve every retained packet's bytes and length; append new datagrams rather than extending its final packet. Defer refill across allocator gaps so each request retains consecutive packet identities. A partial or failed socket attempt discards the remaining plan and retries unsent frame ranges with fresh numbers. Expiry, PMTU shrink and connection retirement cancel every reservation belonging to a discarded plan. Control packets that overtake a retained plan require fresh identities before DATA submission; canceled numbers are never reused.
 
+One bounded owner request pass may split its available burst across ready workers at the same issuance timestamp. Its first grant must meet the ordinary pacing deadline; each additional grant appends its reserved bytes' service time to that deadline. All grants share the existing quantum, window and metadata limits. Continuation requires the same pacing rate and burst cap and ends on the next owner pass or any issuance closure, including an unused completion. Delayed owner turns cannot bank missed service time, and unused reports never rewind the schedule.
+
 Initially pace in userspace using monotonic deadlines and `timerfd`, without requiring an `fq` qdisc or host networking changes. Workers send only with current grants. Cap the combined outstanding burst allowance at the smaller of BBR's send quantum and 64 maximum-size datagrams, spread across workers; GSO aggregates obey that limit too. Timestamp at successful nonblocking socket submission and bound queued socket work. This is a testable baseline; kernel pacing remains a measured optimization. [S11]
 
 ACK-only and PROBE_REPLY traffic can proceed without data-window credit to avoid feedback deadlock, but share a separate 4 MiB/s ceiling per path group with a 16-datagram burst. Coalesce pending ACKs and discard stale feedback when bounded queues fill; do not invent delivery progress. Handshake messages use the per-session one-per-second retry/reply limits and this same control ceiling. This is a ceiling, not a fixed send rate. All these bytes remain visible in counters. Worker/controller scheduling must give bounded service to feedback, fresh frames, and repairs; sender queue expiry drops whole unsent frames where possible.
